  "text": "Questions ten and eleven. Sketch a recommendation or ranking system. And how do you handle the cold-start problem? The standard design is a funnel. Retrieval, a light model searching embeddings for nearest neighbors, narrows millions of items to a few hundred candidates. Ranking, a heavier model with rich cross features, scores each candidate's chance of a click or a purchase. Re-ranking applies business rules: diversity, freshness, promotions. Versions of the ranker are compared in A B tests on revenue or engagement. Two stages, because the heavy model's cost grows with every item it scores. Ten million items is twenty thousand times the work of five hundred, so it only ever sees the five hundred. Cold start is a new user or item with no history, so collaborative filtering has nothing to embed. Fall back to content, like item metadata or preferences asked for at sign-up, or to what's popular. A hybrid blends both signals with learned weights. And a new item that's never shown never earns the clicks that would get it shown: question eight's loop again. So give new items some exposure on purpose.",
